\@writefile{lot}{\contentsline {table}{\numberline {12}{\ignorespaces \textbf  {Every method against every obfuscation, Llama-3.1-8B.} Raw accuracy (strict exact match), with \%\,\emph  {b} after each system: its accuracy as a percentage of \texttt  {base}'s on the same condition. \emph  {base} is the untuned model; \emph  {ICL} its one-shot prompt; \emph  {clean LoRA} is tuned on unobfuscated code; \emph  {breadth} on all five seen transforms; \emph  {anchored} is the paired-consistency objective; \emph  {family} is trained on the unseen family's sibling; \emph  {mixture} is the learned-gate mixture of per-transform specialists; \emph  {merge} is the DARE-TIES merge of them (keys in order: \texttt  {base}, \texttt  {base\_1shot}, \texttt  {tuned\_L0}, \texttt  {mono\_all}, \texttt  {cons\_lam3}, \texttt  {tuned\_X1}, \texttt  {mole\_router}, \texttt  {merge\_dare\_ties}). \textbf  {The backward block is reported twice.} \emph  {by execution} accepts any call that returns the gold value, and inversion is many-to-one, so a guess that lands on a common return value scores; \emph  {by exact arguments} requires the gold call. Measured on CodeLlama-7B, 64--69\,\% of every system's backward successes are of the first kind, which is why the untuned model appears to read backwards almost as well as forwards. $\dagger  $: format-failure rate above 0.25. `--': not run.}}{18}{table.caption.14}\protected@file@percent }
